module discrete_choice

// discrete_choice.ri — IDIOM: keep a binary (+-1) choice IN the model, as an
// auto the solver resolves. The exact form is a Bool auto mapped to a sign,
// plus a constraint that reads it:
//
//     param right : Bool = auto(free)
//     let   side  = if right then 1.0 else -1.0     // exactly +1.0 or -1.0
//     constraint side * side == 1                   // reads `right`
//
// The constraint is not optional. `side * side == 1` always holds here; its
// job is to read `right`, which hands it to the solver. With no constraint
// reading `right`, it stays undef ("awaiting solve"). CP-SAT (default-on since
// #5469) enumerates `right` and resolves `side` to an exact value. Both
// alternatives satisfy `side * side == 1`, so it returns one of them, which is
// not something to rely on, and `auto(free)` warns that the "result is not
// uniquely determined". To choose an alternative, add a constraint that
// discriminates (e.g. `side < 0`). Prefer this form for a genuinely binary
// choice. This file keeps the continuous Real form below, with a dimensionless
// free auto and a quadratic constraint, and documents what that form does and
// does not give you:
//
//     param side : Real = auto(free)
//     constraint side * side == 1        // side is +1 or -1, nothing else
//
// ANTI-PATTERN this replaces: hard-coding one of the two alternatives and
// hand-editing the file to try the other, so the choice never appears in the
// model at all and nothing downstream can reason about it.
//
// SYNTAX — `auto` is legal ONLY as a binding VALUE, never as a declaration
// keyword and never in an operand position:
//     param side : Real = auto(free)     // correct
//     param r    : Length = auto         // correct (strict)
//     let   r    : Length = auto         // correct
//     auto  side : Real                  // PARSE ERROR — "syntax error"
// The last form does not parse: `auto_keyword` is deliberately not a member of
// `_expression` in tree-sitter-reify/grammar.js, so `auto` cannot start a
// member declaration. Verified against a built `reify check`, which rejects
// both `auto side : Real` and `auto fillet_radius : Length` outright.
//
// WHY auto(free) AND NOT STRICT auto — measured, both ways:
//     param side : Real = auto         + constraint side * side == 1
//         -> side = undef, and
//            "error: strict auto parameter resolution is not uniquely
//             determined — consider using auto(free) for exploration"
//     param side : Real = auto(free)   + constraint side * side == 1
//         -> side = -0.9999999999999973
// A strict `auto` triggers a perturbation-based uniqueness re-solve. `s*s == 1`
// has TWO roots by construction, so that re-solve cannot converge and the whole
// parameter goes undef. `auto(free)` skips the uniqueness check. Same reasoning
// as `examples/continuous_cost_min.ri` (see its "Why auto(free), not strict
// auto" note).
//
// BE HONEST ABOUT WHAT THIS DOES: the idiom ENCODES the discrete choice, it
// does not ENUMERATE it. The continuous Nelder-Mead solver converges to ONE
// root from one start (-1 above, not "-1 and +1"). Which root you get is not
// something to rely on. To pin a particular alternative, add a constraint that
// discriminates between them; to explore both, you currently need two runs.
// CP-SAT ships default-on, but it enumerates only finite-domain autos (Bool,
// bounded Int, Enum). `side` is a continuous Real with no finite domain, so
// this component never reaches CP-SAT: the continuous solver solves it and
// returns one root.
//
// Dimensionality: `side` must be dimensionless (`Real`), because it is compared
// against the bare integer `1`. `Real` resolves to a dimensionless scalar, and
// a dimensionless-vs-Int comparison is explicitly permitted — the same
// constraint written against a `Length` would not typecheck.
//
// SHARP EDGE — the resolved root is only approximately +-1
// (-0.9999999999999973, not -1.0), and `==` here is exact float equality. The
// solver's own residual tolerance accepts that at solve time, so this file
// evaluates clean. But feeding `side` into a geometry consumer — e.g.
//     let mount = translate(box(w,w,w), side * arm_len, 0mm, 0mm)
// — adds a post-solve exact re-check of the same constraint, which the residual
// does NOT survive: eval then reports "constraint ... violated" while still
// resolving `side` to -0.999.... Measured directly: identical files with and
// without that one geometry cell differ exactly in whether the violation is
// reported. So keep the +-1 encoding on the value surface, and if a downstream
// consumer needs the sign, give it a tolerance band rather than exact equality,
// or use the Bool form above: it resolves `side` to exactly +-1.0, which
// survives the post-solve exact re-check.

structure def DiscreteChoice {
    // THE IDIOM: one dimensionless free auto, one quadratic constraint.
    // The solver may pick either root; nothing else satisfies the constraint.
    param side : Real = auto(free)
    constraint side * side == 1
}
